<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Rahman I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/731">731</a>–<a href="/years/788">788 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Rahman I was an Umayyad prince who escaped the massacre of his family during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In 756 he established an independent Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, which never recognised Abbasid authority. His survival meant that Umayyad rule continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in the east, limiting the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman I" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_i_21816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…al-Saffah (r. 750–754), inherited the vast Umayyad territorial empire but faced immediate challenges: Shi'a supporters who had expected a dynasty descended from Ali felt betrayed, the Umayyad survivor <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> escaped to establish a rival emirate in al-Andalus, and Abu Muslim himself remained a dangerous independent power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His dynasty descended from <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b>, a survivor of the Umayyad family overthrown by the Abbasids, who had founded an independent emirate at Córdoba in 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous monument, the Great Mosque of Córdoba, was begun by <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> around 785 and enlarged by Abd al-Rahman II and later rulers; its forest of columns and double arches of alternating red brick and white stone became a model for Islamic architecture in the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasids massacred most of the Umayyad family; the most famous survivor, <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b>, escaped to Spain and founded the Emirate of Córdoba in 756.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the reign of <b>Abd al-Rahman I</b> it was absorbed into the Emirate of Córdoba, and in 825 Abd al-Rahman II founded Murcia as the capital of the province of Tudmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/tudmir">Tudmir</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=768" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5477 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abd al-Rahman I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=768">What was here in 768 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
